<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#minnesang">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minnesang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Minnesang was the tradition of courtly love poetry and song in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, flourishing from the twelfth to the fourteenth century. Its poets, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesanger">Minnesänger</a>, were often knights or courtiers who sang of devotion to an idealised noble lady. Leading figures included Reinmar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfram-von-eschenbach">Wolfram von Eschenbach</a>, and much of the poetry survives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-manesse">Codex Manesse</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/minnesang.jpg" alt="Minnesang" width="320" height="487" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister des Codex Manesse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codex_Manesse_Walther_von_der_Vogelweide.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Minnesang, love song, was a tradition of German lyric and song that flourished in the Middle High German period, from the 12th to the 14th century. Its name comes from minne, the Middle High German word for love, its main subject. A poet and performer was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesanger">Minnesänger</a> and a single song a Minnelied. The Minnesänger are comparable to the Occitan troubadours and the northern French trouvères, but are described as an original German contribution to courtly lyric.</p><p>The earliest texts date from perhaps 1150, and the earliest named poets, Der von Kürenberg and Dietmar von Aist, wrote in a native German tradition called the Danubian. From about 1170 German poets came under the influence of the troubadours and trouvères and adopted the strophic form of the canzone; Friedrich von Hausen's Ich denke underwilen is regarded as a contrafactum of a song by Guiot de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/provins">Provins</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Debate about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesanger">Minnesänger</a>'s social status rests on little reliable biography. Some belonged to the higher nobility: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-manesse">Codex Manesse</a> includes songs by dukes, counts, kings and the Emperor Henry VI. Others were educated commoners, as the title Meister shows, such as Konrad von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wurzburg">Würzburg</a>, and many are thought to have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ministerialis">ministeriales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of lower nobility. They wrote for their own class at court and are better thought of as courtiers than as hired musicians. Friedrich von Hausen was in the entourage of Friedrich Barbarossa and died on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a> received a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> from the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>. Several, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henric-van-veldeke">Heinrich von Veldeke</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfram-von-eschenbach">Wolfram von Eschenbach</a> and Hartmann von Aue, are also noted for epic poetry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>From about 1190 the poets broke free of Franco-Provençal influence. Classical Minnesang, with Albrecht von Johansdorf, Heinrich von Morungen and Reinmar von Hagenau, reached its culmination in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a>, regarded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and today as the foremost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesanger">Minnesänger</a>. The later Minnesang, from about 1230, turned partly away from the refined ethos and grew formally more elaborate; Neidhart von Reuental brought in characters from lower social classes, often for humour. Only a small number of melodies survive, mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the 15th century or later, and their rhythm is hard to read. In the 15th century Minnesang gave way to the Meistersänger, who were usually commoners. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>'s Tannhäuser and Richard Strauss's Guntram are operas about the tradition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Minnesang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Babenbergs acquired Styria in 1192 and made Vienna a centre of <b>Minnesang</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-austria">Duchy of Austria</a></li><li><blockquote>Its knightly culture produced <b>Minnesang</b> poets.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-swabia">Duchy of Swabia</a></li><li><blockquote>Landgrave Hermann I made the Wartburg a centre of <b>Minnesang</b>, hosting poets such as Walther von der Vogelweide and Wolfram von Eschenbach; this was later remembered as the Wartburg contest of song.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-thuringia">Landgraviate of Thuringia</a></li></ul></section>
